# `times` in an interactive login bash on Ubuntu 22.04 and in the dash behind /bin/sh: the two
# lines of CPU time (the shell's own, then its children's), `0m0.000s` in bash and `0m0.000000s` in
# dash, all zero in a subshell, a command substitution or a fresh `sh -c`; bash takes no option
# (`times: -x: invalid option`, usage line, status 2) and ignores operands, dash ignores both;
# `times` is a shell builtin in bash and a special one in dash. The figures of a long-running login
# shell vary from run to run, so only the cases that are always zero are recorded here. Each reply
# was produced by `bash --noprofile --norc --noediting -i` started as a login shell (`exec -a -bash`)
# in the localhost/propolis-survey-ref:jammy container (bash 5.1.16, dash 0.5.11), run with
# `--network none`, PS1 and PS2 set to markers and these lines fed one at a time on standard input.
# source: ubuntu:22.04 container run of bash 5.1.16 and dash 0.5.11 (synthetic command shapes)
# date: 2026-10-10
# protocol: ssh
# persona: ubuntu
$ times -x
> -bash: times: -x: invalid option
> times: usage: times
$ echo $?
> 2
$ times foo >/dev/null
$ echo $?
> 0
$ (times)
> 0m0.000s 0m0.000s
> 0m0.000s 0m0.000s
$ echo $(times)
> 0m0.000s 0m0.000s 0m0.000s 0m0.000s
$ sh -c 'times'
> 0m0.000000s 0m0.000000s
> 0m0.000000s 0m0.000000s
$ sh -c 'times; echo $?'
> 0m0.000000s 0m0.000000s
> 0m0.000000s 0m0.000000s
> 0
$ sh -c 'times -x; echo $?'
> 0m0.000000s 0m0.000000s
> 0m0.000000s 0m0.000000s
> 0
$ sh -c 'times foo; echo $?'
> 0m0.000000s 0m0.000000s
> 0m0.000000s 0m0.000000s
> 0
$ sh -c '/bin/true; /bin/true; times'
> 0m0.000000s 0m0.000000s
> 0m0.000000s 0m0.000000s
$ sh -c 'sleep 1; times'
> 0m0.000000s 0m0.000000s
> 0m0.000000s 0m0.000000s
$ sh -c 'type times'
> times is a special shell builtin
$ type times
> times is a shell builtin
$ command -v times
> times
$ times >/dev/null; echo $?
> 0
